Good - I can see that `move_object` changes the zone field of the object within `state.objects` but does NOT remove the object from `state.objects`. So `state.get_object(object_id)` will still find the Noble after it moves to the graveyard — it will be found with `zone == Zone::Graveyard`.

For `on_dies` in Falkenrath Noble:
```rust
fn on_dies(&self, state: &mut GameState, object_id: ObjectId, _registry: &CardRegistry) {
    let controller = state.get_object(object_id).map(|o| o.controller).unwrap_or(PlayerId(0));
    drain(state, controller);
}
```
Since `get_object` finds the Noble in the graveyard with the correct controller, this works fine.

For `on_any_creature_dies`, the implementation checks that the object is still on the battlefield before triggering the drain effect — if it's not there, the handler returns early without doing anything.

Now I'm seeing the real problems. The "target player" language in the oracle text means the controller should get to choose which player loses life, but the current `drain()` function just auto-targets the opponent with no choice involved. And when Falkenrath Noble dies simultaneously with another creature, the trigger should fire twice according to the rules, but I need to trace through how the state machine handles that scenario — when Noble gets destroyed, it moves to the graveyard before the other creature dies, so the trigger fires once for the other creature's death. so the death-watch trigger for Noble watching OtherCreature's death won't fire because Noble is already in the graveyard by the time that event is processed. This means simultaneous deaths would only generate one trigger instead of two, which contradicts the ruling. I need to dig into how the engine actually processes state-based actions to see if all creatures are moved to the graveyard before or after trigger collection happens. Checking the death-watch trigger collection logic more carefully — it filters for permanents currently on the battlefield, so Noble in the graveyard won't match and won't trigger its death-watch ability when OtherCreature dies. So there IS an issue with the simultaneous death ruling. Now I need to check how the engine actually processes state-based actions and trigger collection to see if triggers are gathered before or after creatures move to the graveyard.

